// 283 — panic / recover (Go-style) — DESIGN NOTE
// Go separates "recoverable" errors (returned values, type `error`)
// from "the program is in a broken state" (`panic("...")`).
// `recover()` inside a deferred function lets a goroutine catch its
// own panic and convert it back into a normal error — useful for
// keeping a server alive when one request handler explodes.
//
// AiLang has no panic/recover. The equivalent boundary is much more
// rigid:
//
//   * Recoverable errors → negative-sentinel returns (281, 282).
//   * "Program is broken" → HaltProgram(exit_code), no recovery.
//
// There is no in-process catch-the-bad-thing-and-keep-running. If
// you need that property (e.g. a request server) the model is
// process supervision: each unit of work runs as its own process,
// the supervisor restarts crashed children. Same fault isolation,
// at OS-process granularity instead of in-process try-blocks.
SubRoutine.Main {
    PrintMessage("AiLang has no panic/recover. The boundaries are:\n")
    PrintMessage("  recoverable failures  -> sentinel returns (see 281, 282)\n")
    PrintMessage("  broken-state crashes  -> HaltProgram(exit_code), terminal\n")
    PrintMessage("  process-level recovery -> use OS process supervision\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
